{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/3542-you-know-what-i-love","id":3542,"title":"You Know What I Love?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Additional Discussion","Off-Topic"],"posts":[{"date":"2013-11-05T09:01:10-08:00","id":3542,"title":"You Know What I Love?","content":"This thread is for sharing the little--or big--triumphs and joys of life to a receptive audience. I'll get the ball rolling:<br /> <br /> You know what I love?<br /> <br /> I have these two friends. Neither of them live anywhere near me, but we met online and they, being braver souls than I am (I'd have risked the sort of rejection they did, but only after another year of knowing them), invited me to come meet them, so I did, and, well, it was awesome. It was one of the best things I've ever been done. It was a life-changer. It was also two years ago, so it's not what I'm talking about; I just bring it up to set the stage.<br /> <br /> Every now and then they surprise me with some kindness. Last week, they invited me to come up to spend Thanksgiving with them. I'd have loved to do so, and for once I'm financially stable enough that I could have paid for the trip myself, so that would have been great too. Unfortunately, I couldn't take them up on the offer due to family politics--my gran, who used to host Thanksgiving for the whole family, died this August, and the inheritance issue has been extremely acrimonious, so my sister is hosting Thanksgiving for those members of the family we don't want to cut ties with, and as such I have to show up for this one or else send a really wrong message--but that's not the point. The point is, it was so sweet of them, and I love them for it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/271-jimb","name":"JimB","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar271_2.jpeg","t